shadowfetch-phoenix (2.1.2-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Initial release for Shadowfetch Linux 2.1.2 "Fire Edition": the safety
    pillar as its own package.
  * phoenix-firstboot: on a Btrfs root, create the snapper root config the
    way our Calamares layout needs it (dedicated top-level @snapshots kept,
    the nested .snapshots subvolume create-config insists on is deleted and
    the fstab mount put back), tune it (TIMELINE_CREATE=no, NUMBER_LIMIT=8,
    NUMBER_LIMIT_IMPORTANT=4, EMPTY_PRE_POST_CLEANUP=yes, FREE_LIMIT=0.2,
    QGROUP unset), grant the first user's group D-Bus read access, enable
    snapper-cleanup.timer and grub-btrfsd, and take Point #1 "Fresh
    install". Guarded off live media, exactly like shadowfetch-firstboot.
  * phoenix-restore: crash-atomic rollback. Snapshot the Point to @new,
    sync, then one renameat2(RENAME_EXCHANGE) via mv --exchange - never a
    window with no @ subvolume, so GRUB's baked /@/boot/grub prefix survives
    a power cut at any point. Explicitly NOT `snapper rollback` (that needs
    the openSUSE default-subvolume layout we do not use). Supported from
    inside the "Last Known Good Flame" overlay session: the Point being
    ridden is preselected from /run/phoenix-overlay and all writes go to the
    real volume mounted from subvolid=5.
  * phoenix-postboot: paired oneshot that runs update-grub when the restore
    flag exists and garbage-collects @_prev/@new leftovers beyond the most
    recent, skipped inside overlay sessions.
  * phoenix-space-check + apt.conf.d/79phoenix-space (sorts before
    snapper's 80snapper): below 10% free it synchronously runs snapper
    number-cleanup and GCs stale @_prev subvolumes; if still below 2 GiB it
    posts a desktop warning naming the number before the transaction
    proceeds. Never blocks or fails the transaction.
  * phoenix-desktop-reset: user-scope reset of a curated Plasma 6 config
    list to a timestamped backup, personal files untouched and not even on
    the list; undoable by construction (--undo), restarts plasmashell.
  * phoenix-recovery-report: privacy-safe local diagnostic tarball
    (journal errors without hostnames, apt history, Point list, dmesg,
    lsblk, btrfs usage, SMART JSON, failed units). Nothing is uploaded.
  * polkit actions org.shadowfetch.phoenix.restore and
    org.shadowfetch.phoenix.recovery-report (auth_admin_keep for the active
    console user); listing Points needs no polkit at all (snapperd
    ALLOW_GROUPS read access set at first boot).
  * Overlay-session banner autostart deep-links Control Center ->
    Phoenix -> Restore at the ridden Point (shadowfetch-control --page
    recover:point=N).

 -- Shadowfetch Project <signing@shadowfetch.com>  Thu, 30 Jul 2026 12:00:00 -0400
